Forward the pointer cursor's name to the host.

The launcher's Display page opens the on-screen keyboard when the user taps a text field inside
the X session, and nothing on the Java side can tell that a tap landed on a text field: the host
sees a SurfaceView full of pixels, and X tells it nothing about focus or what is under the
pointer. What the server does know is which cursor the window under the pointer asked for, and
the Xcursor names of a text caret — "xterm", "text", "ibeam", "vertical-text" — are exactly that
signal. The name lives in `CursorRec.name`, an Atom in the server's own atom table. XFixes does
publish it, but only to X clients (`XFixesSelectCursorInput`, `XFixesGetCursorName`), and the
host is not one; making it one would mean a second X connection and libX11 inside the app process
to learn something the DDX already has in hand.

So the DDX reports it. `lorie.h` gains `EVENT_CURSOR_NAME_CHANGED`, whose variable-length payload
works exactly like `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_SEND`'s, and both ends clamp the name to
`LORIE_CURSOR_NAME_MAX`. `lorieSetCursor` resolves `pCurs->name` through `ValidAtom`/`NameForAtom`
— no cursor, or one with no name, is the empty string — before the oversize substitution below it
can swap the cursor out, and sends only when the name changed, so a pointer crossing windows that
share a cursor stays silent. `lorieActivityConnected` repeats the current name, because a host
attaching to a running session would otherwise not learn it until the cursor next changed. All of
this runs on the X server thread, the thread that writes `conn_fd` everywhere else (see
`DDXNotifyFocusChanged` and `lorieSendClipboardData`), and a name produced while nothing is
attached is dropped. `activity.cpp` reads the payload and calls
`LorieView.onCursorNameChanged(String)`, resolved in `nativeInit` beside `resetIme`.

Applies after 0001-look-up-the-host-class-as-LorieHost.patch, whose `nativeInit` hunk this one
extends. `LorieView` must carry the `@Keep onCursorNameChanged(String)` method or
`FindMethodOrDie` kills the process on the first view, so the Java half ships in the same commit
as this patch and the prebuilt built from it.

See x11-server/UPSTREAM.md.

